WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our next guests
are the writers and producers

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.903
behind the movie "Dumb Money,"

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.238
which is playing
in theaters now.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:06.472
Let's take a look.

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:08.508
-Hey, do you -- do you remember
back at Stonehill

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.944
when they dared me
to run that mile naked?

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:12.578
-Yeah, there was a crazy storm
that night.

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:14.213
-Everybody remembers that.
-Okay.

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:15.882
-They do, man.
You don't think people remember

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:17.216
your four-minute,
three-second mile?

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:20.420
-Oh, what is this, a pep talk?
-Stop hiding.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.854
Seriously, okay?

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:24.691
Stop being all meek and [bleep]
and running away.

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:26.192
-What, you want me to run
through lightning

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:28.093
with my [bleep] out?
-Yeah.

00:00:28.093 --> 00:00:30.930
Please. Exactly that.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.365
Run through lightning
with your [bleep] out.

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:33.967
[Bleep] it.

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.302
-Please welcome to the show
Lauren Schuker Blum

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.471
and Rebecca Angelo, everybody!

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:40.506
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.506 --> 00:00:57.590 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.525
Welcome! I'm so happy
to have you both here.

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:00.826
-We're happy to be here.

00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:02.595
-And, you know, we don't often
have two guests at once,

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.498
but you are the best
coordinated outfits

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:07.233
I've ever seen on the show.
-We tried.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.269
-You tried very hard,
and it paid off big time.

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:11.070
-She's in charge of outfits.
I just listen.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.138
-Yeah.

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:15.175
-So, I think
the GameStop phenomenon

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:16.809
was something
we were all aware of,

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:19.545
but maybe didn't fully
understand the details of.

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:21.814
For those who weren't fully
up to speed on it,

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:24.784
can you just explain what
happened in that moment at time?

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:26.786
-Yeah, it's David and Goliath.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:31.023
It is the insane, true story
of thousands of regular people

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:34.027
who came together and beat
Wall Street at its own game.

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:36.862
-And they were led
by this guy Keith Gill,

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:38.431
who went by the name
Roaring Kitty

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.099
and Deep [bleep] Value online.

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:41.500
He had two names.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.838
And he is...you know,
he's funny, he's weird.

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:48.975
He did his YouTube videos in cat
T-shirts with a red headband.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:52.178
And he made fifty --
almost $50 million overnight

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.814
from GameStop, that
video game store at the mall

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:57.483
that we went to
as nerdy eight-year-olds.

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:58.918
-So, the thing about GameStop is

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:01.353
hedge funds
had shorted the company,

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:03.623
which meant that
if the business failed

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:05.758
and everybody who worked there
lost their jobs,

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:08.427
the hedge fund guys
would get rich.

00:02:08.427 --> 00:02:11.263
But Keith and thousands
or even millions of other people

00:02:11.263 --> 00:02:14.267
bought into the stock,
which sent the price soaring.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.102
A bunch of them
got rich in the process,

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:18.404
and a few hedge funds
went under.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:20.240
-And dumb money,
I did not realize,

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.042
is an actual term
that Wall Street uses

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:25.211
about the kind of investor
that Keith was.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.679
-Right. Well, Wall Street
calls itself smart money.

00:02:27.679 --> 00:02:29.482
Real, you know, original.

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:32.285
And they call retail investors
dumb money.

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:34.987
-So, in the moment
where the dumb money

00:02:34.987 --> 00:02:36.322
was buying up GameStop,

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:37.824
they did not take it
seriously at first,

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:40.559
because they thought this just
proved their actual thesis,

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:43.563
which is that if we're on
the right side of this deal,

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:44.897
that has to be...
-Exactly.

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.665
The whole story
caught everyone by surprise.

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:48.734
-Yeah, they call it --
they call it a herding event.

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:52.071
So, like, literally they view
regular people as cattle.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:55.474
So, did not take it seriously
until it was way too late.

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:58.010
-You guys met writing
for the Wall Street Journal.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.613
And how, when you write
a screenplay like this,

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.448
how does your background
as reporters help

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.183
in, you know,
putting this story together?

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:06.686
-It's so helpful. I think
we're still reporters at heart,

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:09.255
and we interviewed
so many people on Wall Street,

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.690
we talked to retail traders,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.792
and pretty much everything
in the movie is true,

00:03:13.792 --> 00:03:15.528
except for one thing.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.397
Pete Davidson,
who stars in the film,

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:19.832
plays a real guy
named Kevin Gill,

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:21.500
and Kevin is a DoorDasher.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.502
He's an excellent DoorDasher
with a --

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:24.904
-The real Kevin is an excellent
DoorDasher.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.739
He has a 99% rating.
-Okay.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.107
-But when we cast
Pete Davidson,

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.442
we realized we can't sell
Pete Davidson

00:03:30.442 --> 00:03:32.945
as an excellent DoorDasher.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:36.115
So we made him a DoorDasher
from hell in the movie.

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:37.450
-Gotcha.
-He eats people's food,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.185
he drinks their drinks.

00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:41.954
He's driving his car
all over their lawns.

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:44.623
-Now, you guys have been
up against something

00:03:44.623 --> 00:03:45.858
that a lot of films
have been up about.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.694
You have this incredible cast --

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:51.130
Paul Dano, Nick Offerman,
Seth Rogen...

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.132
-Sebastian Stan.
-America Ferrera.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.467
-It's a great cast,

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.636
but unfortunately,
no one is able to go out

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:58.170
because of the SAG strike
and do press.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:59.972
Which means that
as writers and producers,

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:02.976
you have been doing a great deal
of press for this movie.

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:05.111
Has that been
a strange experience?

00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:06.912
-Yes. [ Laughs ]

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:08.981
-Yeah. I wouldn't say...

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:11.617
We're, like, not supposed to be
brought into direct sunlight.

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:13.686
We're supposed to be
kept in, like, a closet.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:16.088
-Yeah, writers are, like, behind
their laptops, hunched over.

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:18.757
And here we are
on national television.

00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:20.626
You know, we were at
the Toronto Film Festival.

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:23.596
We pulled up to the red carpet
in our car

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.130
and we could hear
the crowd going crazy.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.465
They were like,
"Yes, it's Seth Rogen!"

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.634
And then the doors open,
and the crowd was like, "Oh."

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.469
They got us.

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.305
-You did take a lovely photo
on the red carpet.

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:33.372
Like, don't
sell yourself short.

00:04:33.372 --> 00:04:35.041
-We're trying so hard.

00:04:35.041 --> 00:04:37.043
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:40.313
-Those bags, our friend

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.415
who designs Edie Parker
made those for us.

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:44.784
And they say "dumb" on one side
and "money" on the other.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:47.219
And we got out of our car
so confidently

00:04:47.219 --> 00:04:49.922
and held up our bags and
were smiling for the cameras.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:53.526
And then somebody ran up to us
and switched around Lauren's bag

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:55.027
because she was flashing "dumb."

00:04:55.027 --> 00:04:56.428
So, we were like idiots,

00:04:56.428 --> 00:04:58.164
just like smiling
for the camera,

00:04:58.164 --> 00:05:00.799
And it just said, "Dumb dumb."

00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:02.902
-You're like, "We're famous!
Dumb dumb!"

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:04.737
-Yes.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:07.740
-Your husband, Jason Blum,
who's a film producer,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.208
he was actually here last week.
-He was.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:10.576
-And he was talking
about the fact

00:05:10.576 --> 00:05:11.910
that he had a movie
coming out,

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.312
and then he had
to change the date

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:15.248
because Taylor Swift
had a movie coming out.

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:17.383
He didn't want to open
the same weekend as her.

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:19.017
-Right.
-But then the issue was

00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:20.519
that he might have
to move to the weekend

00:05:20.519 --> 00:05:22.688
your movie was coming out,
and he said that was very tense.

00:05:22.688 --> 00:05:23.990
-Yeah. Jerk.
-Yeah.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.925
-Yeah, he stole our weekend.

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:27.092
[ Laughter ]

00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:28.795
-So, ultimately,
did it all work out okay?

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:30.463
-Things are going okay, yes.

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:32.631
But he had a competing project
to ours, also.

00:05:32.631 --> 00:05:34.000
-Yeah.
-His own...

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.135
-Oh, he had his own...
-He had his own GameStop movie.

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:39.004
So we had a kind of, like,
Cold War in our house.

00:05:39.004 --> 00:05:41.007
And every time the studio
called with notes,

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:44.343
I had to, like, hide
in the closet to take the notes.

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:45.744
And then I would open
the door afterwards

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:47.446
and I would hit Jason
in the face

00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:49.748
'cause he was waiting outside,
eavesdropping.

00:05:49.748 --> 00:05:51.751
-How could he do that
to his own wife?

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:53.953
-And our children were like
spies somehow, you know.

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:55.621
It was a tough 18 months.
-Alright.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:57.490
I'm glad that you came out
the winner

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.158
on the GameStop saga.
-The important thing is we won.

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:00.326
-Yes, you did win.

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:01.827
And it's really
a wonderful movie,

00:06:01.827 --> 00:06:03.195
and I hope that people
go out and see it.

00:06:03.195 --> 00:06:04.696
I enjoyed it a great deal,
and it's so lovely

00:06:04.696 --> 00:06:06.365
to have you both here.
Thank you so much.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:10.035
You guys,
that's Lauren Schuker Blum

00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:11.537
and Rebecca Angelo, everybody.

00:06:11.537 --> 00:06:13.005
"Dumb Money"
is in theaters now.

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:14.374
Be right back
with more "Late Night."

